---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SavingsChart from '../components/SavingsChart.astro';
import data from '../data/benchmark-results.json';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const m = data.metadata;
const h = data.headline;
const fmt = (n: number) => n.toLocaleString('en-US');
const pct = (v: number | null) => (v === null ? '—' : `${v >= 0 ? '' : '−'}${Math.round(Math.abs(v) * 100)}%`);
const savedTokens = h.totalBaselineTokens - h.totalToolTokens;

const label: Record<string, string> = {
  outline: 'File outline', 'symbol-info': 'Symbol info (metadata)',
  'symbol-info-source': 'Symbol info (+source)', references: 'Find references',
  implementations: 'Find implementations', 'call-graph': 'Call graph (callers)',
  'edit-output': 'Rename → diff',
};
const chartIds = ['outline', 'symbol-info', 'symbol-info-source', 'references', 'implementations', 'call-graph'];
const chartRows = data.suites.filter((s) => chartIds.includes(s.id)).map((s) => ({
  id: s.id, tool: s.tool, title: label[s.id] ?? s.title,
  value: s.aggregate.medianSavingsVsWholeFile, min: s.aggregate.minSavingsVsWholeFile,
  max: s.aggregate.maxSavingsVsWholeFile, pooled: s.aggregate.pooledSavingsVsWholeFile, n: s.aggregate.count,
}));
const editSuite = data.suites.find((s) => s.id === 'edit-output');
---
<Base title="Benchmark — how many tokens RoselineMCP saves" active="benchmark"
      description="An honest, reproducible measurement of the token savings from RoselineMCP's navigation tools, run against its own source.">
  <h1>How many tokens do these tools save?</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    An honest, reproducible measurement — not a marketing number. Every figure is a real string:
    the tool’s actual JSON output versus the source an agent would otherwise read, tokenized with a
    real BPE tokenizer. The suites are <strong>systematic sweeps</strong> over RoselineMCP’s own
    source, so the distribution is representative — weak cases included.
  </p>

  <div class="stats">
    <div class="tile"><div class="n save">{Math.round(h.pooledSavingsReadTools * 100)}%</div><div class="l">pooled reduction<br/>(size-weighted)</div></div>
    <div class="tile"><div class="n">{Math.round(h.medianSavingsReadTools * 100)}%</div><div class="l">median per task</div></div>
    <div class="tile"><div class="n">{fmt(savedTokens)}</div><div class="l">tokens saved<br/>on the sample</div></div>
    <div class="tile"><div class="n">{fmt(h.totalBaselineTokens)}</div><div class="l">baseline tokens<br/>(reading the files)</div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="meta-row">
    <span><b>Target:</b> {m.solution} · {m.targetProject}</span>
    <span><b>Files swept:</b> {m.filesSwept}</span>
    <span><b>Symbols swept:</b> {m.symbolsSwept}</span>
    <span><b>Tokenizer:</b> cl100k_base</span>
    <span><b>Commit:</b> <code>{m.commit}</code></span>
    <span><b>Date:</b> {m.generatedAt}</span>
  </div>

  <h2>Savings by tool</h2>
  <SavingsChart rows={chartRows} />

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Where the file outline wins — and where it can’t</h3>
    <p>The first run of this benchmark caught <code>search_symbols</code> outline <em>costing</em>
      tokens: it repeated the identical file path and the fully-qualified name on every symbol. That
      was fixed — the outline now returns a lean projection (name, kind, signature, line), and the
      median went from <strong>−45%</strong> to <strong>+30%</strong>.</p>
    <p>What remains is not a bug but a limit. Outline wins big on body-heavy files, where it strips
      the implementations (<code>Program.cs</code> <span class="save-ink">+89%</span>,
      <code>CodeFixService.cs</code> <span class="save-ink">+76%</span>). On a declaration-only file
      — an interface or a DTO — it stays near break-even or slightly negative
      (<code>IDiagnosticFilterService.cs</code> <span class="cost-ink">−92%</span>), because that
      file <em>is</em> already just signatures: there is no body to strip, so a structured list of
      the same signatures can’t be smaller. Read such files directly; use the outline on the big ones.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Full results</h2>
  <p>Two baselines. <strong>B1 (whole-file)</strong> = the full file(s) an agent must open — the
    realistic-but-generous baseline. <strong>B2 (targeted)</strong> = only the relevant lines ±3
    (a <code>grep -C3</code> model) — a conservative lower bound. “Weak” counts tasks that saved
    under 25% vs B1.</p>
  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Tool</th><th>Scenario</th><th class="num">n</th>
          <th class="num">median</th><th class="num">pooled</th>
          <th class="num">range (min…max)</th><th class="num">vs grep (B2)</th><th class="num">weak</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {data.suites.map((s) => {
          const a = s.aggregate;
          const good = a.medianSavingsVsWholeFile >= 0.25;
          return (
            <tr>
              <td class="mono">{s.tool}</td>
              <td>{s.title}{!s.systematic && <span class="pill" style="margin-left:.4rem">illustrative</span>}</td>
              <td class="num">{a.count}</td>
              <td class={`num ${good ? 'save-ink' : 'cost-ink'}`}>{pct(a.medianSavingsVsWholeFile)}</td>
              <td class={`num ${a.pooledSavingsVsWholeFile >= 0.25 ? 'save-ink' : 'cost-ink'}`}>{pct(a.pooledSavingsVsWholeFile)}</td>
              <td class="num muted">{pct(a.minSavingsVsWholeFile)}…{pct(a.maxSavingsVsWholeFile)}</td>
              <td class="num muted">{pct(a.medianSavingsVsTargeted)}</td>
              <td class="num muted">{a.weakOrNegativeCount}/{a.count}</td>
            </tr>
          );
        })}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Reading the two baselines honestly</h3>
    <ul class="clean">
      <li><strong>vs. reading whole files (B1):</strong> the read tools cut 54–91% — because a file
        is far bigger than the one fact you needed from it.</li>
      <li><strong>vs. an optimal grep (B2):</strong> the tools are roughly <em>comparable</em> in
        raw tokens (e.g. <code>find_references</code> ≈ <code>grep -C3</code>; <code>get_symbol_info</code>
        metadata can even exceed a tiny member’s own text). Their advantage over grep is
        <em>structure and precision</em> — resolved symbols, cross-project references, real call
        edges — not raw token count. Real agent behavior sits between the two, so both are shown.</li>
      <li><strong><code>get_symbol_info</code> with <code>includeSource=true</code></strong> is shown
        separately (~55%): you get the declaration body back, so savings shrink and can go negative
        for tiny members. That’s the point — the tool doesn’t pretend to replace reading code you’re
        about to edit.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  {editSuite && (
    <div class="note">
      <h3>Output tokens: emitting a diff instead of rewriting files</h3>
      <p>A different axis — the tokens an agent must <em>emit</em> to change code.
        <code>rename_symbol</code> returns a unified diff instead of the full text of every file it
        touches: a pooled <strong class="save-ink">{pct(editSuite.aggregate.pooledSavingsVsWholeFile)}</strong>
        reduction across {editSuite.aggregate.count} illustrative renames.</p>
    </div>
  )}

  <h2>Methodology</h2>
  <ul class="clean">{data.methodology.map((x: string) => <li>{x}</li>)}</ul>

  <h2>Limitations</h2>
  <ul class="clean">{data.limitations.map((x: string) => <li>{x}</li>)}</ul>

  <h2>Reproduce it</h2>
  <p>The harness loads the solution once via MSBuild, runs the real services, tokenizes their
    output, and writes the JSON this page renders. Run it against your own solution to get your
    numbers:</p>
  <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/Atypical-Consulting/RoselineMCP
cd RoselineMCP
dotnet run --project RoselineMCP.TokenBenchmark -c Release</code></pre>
  <p class="muted">Output: <code>website/src/data/benchmark-results.json</code>, stamped with the
    commit and date. Tokenizer: {m.tokenizer}. Tool output format: {m.toolOutputFormat}.</p>
</Base>
